# **The gauntlet's work order.** One line per violation, as
# `room | rule | offender` — see `gauntlet.rs`, "The docket".
#
# This file is the deliverable of the harness pass and the input to the
# fixing pass. Strike a line, run `cargo test -p cabin`, and
# `the_gauntlet_finds_exactly_the_docket` will tell you whether the thing
# is actually gone: a line struck too early fails, and a defect fixed
# without striking its line fails too.
#
# Regenerate wholesale with:
#   ./target/debug/space-trucking --gauntlet-docket
# and read the numbers behind any line with:
#   ./target/debug/space-trucking --gauntlet
#
# Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored.
#
# **It is empty, and the work order is worked through.**
#
# The lines it carried were three families and one cause: every cell of
# every chart was a berth cargo could stay in, so a room owned no volume
# of its own and a station's furniture was a defect wherever it stood.
# A calling room keeps its own deck now — `Tile::Staging`, which cargo
# may be set down on and nothing stays on (docs/ROOMS.md, "The staging
# law") — and the three rules ask about the berths that are left: the
# room's goods, a proposal's chalk, a doorway, the counter's own cell,
# and the plain deck of the rooms that ride.
#
# Narrowing them retired 289 findings of 447, and none of the 289 was
# ever a defect: each was a station's fitting standing in air the room
# had no other use for. The remaining 158 were the honest half, and they
# were fixed by moving furniture — every station's hardware came off its
# own shopfront and off the chalk, onto its flanks and the middle of its
# own deck, which is where a shop keeps its fittings anyway.
#
# What a defect looks like from here is a line appearing in this file.
